{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 7, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the County of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1199. He was a significant figure in the Crusades a powerful and influential ruler known for his military prowess diplomatic skill and economic prosperity. Born around 1134 Raymond inherited the County at a young age upon the death of his father Raymond II. His early reign was marked by consolidating his power dealing with internal rebellions and maintaining alliances with neighboring rulers.\n\n*Early Reign and Consolidation of Power*\n\nRaymond faced numerous challenges early in his reign including opposition from local barons and attempts by the Zengid dynasty to exert control over Tripoli. He skillfully navigated these threats employing a combination of diplomacy military force and strategic marriages. Raymond strengthened the county's defenses rebuilt fortifications and fostered economic growth particularly through trade. He also engaged in complex political maneuvering balancing the interests of the Crusader states with those of Muslim rulers. His marriage to Sibylla of Tuscany solidified his position and provided him with crucial support. He actively worked to maintain a fragile peace with Saladin a key figure in the Muslim world.\n\nRaymond III played a crucial role in the Third Crusade. He provided military support to King Richard the Lionheart and other Crusader forces in the Levant. He offered supplies troops and logistical assistance helping to sustain the Crusader effort against Saladin. His strategic location and military resources made him a valuable ally. While not directly participating in major battles Raymond's contributions were essential to the overall Crusader campaign. He also engaged in negotiations with Saladin seeking to secure favorable terms for the Crusader states.\n\n*Diplomacy and Relations with Saladin*\n\nRaymond's relationship with Saladin was particularly noteworthy. Unlike some of his contemporaries Raymond maintained a relatively pragmatic and diplomatic approach to Saladin. He recognized the need for coexistence and sought to avoid unnecessary conflict. He engaged in regular correspondence with Saladin and at times even negotiated truces and agreements. This approach allowed Tripoli to remain relatively stable and prosperous even during the height of the Crusades. He understood the importance of maintaining trade relations and facilitating cultural exchange. His diplomatic efforts prevented widespread devastation in his county.\n\n*Legacy and Death*\n\nRaymond III died in 1199 leaving behind a prosperous and relatively stable County of Tripoli. He was remembered as a capable ruler a shrewd diplomat and a skilled military leader. His reign is often viewed as a high point in the history of the County of Tripoli a period of economic growth political stability and cultural flourishing. His policies contributed to the county's independence and its ability to withstand the pressures of the Crusades. He is considered one of the most successful and influential Counts of Tripoli a testament to his leadership and political acumen. His death led to a period of instability and eventually the fall of the County of Tripoli to Saladin in 1198.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0021344278939068317}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -3.099436753473128e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.29277363419532776}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.39782649278640747}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.15701399743556976}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.0002329078270122409}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -2.0265373677830212e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -8.702239938429557e-06}